1
00:00:07,757 --> 00:00:09,843
[engine turns over, revs]

2
00:00:11,678 --> 00:00:16,266
[Jerry] This is a 1961 Cadillac Series 62
convertible.

3
00:00:16,349 --> 00:00:19,978
Cadillacs have nothing to do with reality.
What year is it?

4
00:00:20,061 --> 00:00:22,772
It's a '61. What do we call it? '62.

5
00:00:23,189 --> 00:00:24,733
Sometimes you want to stretch out,

6
00:00:24,816 --> 00:00:27,152
you want to open up,
you want to spread your wings.

7
00:00:27,235 --> 00:00:29,112
That's when you need a Cadillac like this

8
00:00:29,195 --> 00:00:33,158
in Pompeian red
with a maroon with white interior.

9
00:00:33,408 --> 00:00:38,288
It's got a 6.4 liter V8, 325 horsepower,

10
00:00:38,371 --> 00:00:43,334
weighs almost 5,000 pounds,
and goes 125 miles an hour.

11
00:00:43,418 --> 00:00:46,087
Cadillac marketed this model as, quote,

12
00:00:46,171 --> 00:00:49,257
"The new standard of the world
in supremacy!"

13
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:52,635
That's about 15 years after World War II.
Really? World supremacy?

14
00:00:53,178 --> 00:00:54,387
Is that good marketing?

15
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:56,681
If you crash this car,

16
00:00:56,765 --> 00:01:00,226
you really just wanna fly through the air
in a swan dive and end it.

17
00:01:00,477 --> 00:01:03,521
But the funeral people would go,
"Wow, that guy had a lot of style."

18
00:01:04,564 --> 00:01:07,650
Which is why it's the perfect car
for my very special guest today,

19
00:01:07,734 --> 00:01:09,444
Bridget Everett,

20
00:01:09,527 --> 00:01:10,445
and Poppy,

21
00:01:10,528 --> 00:01:14,699
one of the most exciting night club
cabaret performers I have ever seen.

22
00:01:14,783 --> 00:01:17,368
I'm not making any sense.
Cut that out of the footage.

23
00:01:17,452 --> 00:01:20,038
[Jerry] I don't really know how
to describe what it is that she does

24
00:01:20,121 --> 00:01:21,664
but I've seen it, I love it.

25
00:01:21,748 --> 00:01:24,667
She's exciting. She's hilarious.
She's musical.

26
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:26,920
It's a show
that's completely unique to her.

27
00:01:27,003 --> 00:01:30,173
You go see Bridget Everett,
you will never forget it.

28
00:01:30,256 --> 00:01:31,549
I like people like that.

29
00:01:32,008 --> 00:01:35,220
She describes herself
as an old cabaret provocateur.

30
00:01:35,428 --> 00:01:38,431
Part of her show is on stage,
part of it is in the audience,

31
00:01:38,515 --> 00:01:40,892
very interactive
whether you like it or not.

32
00:01:41,434 --> 00:01:43,144
-Bridget?
-[Bridget] Hello, Jerry?

33
00:01:43,228 --> 00:01:46,272
[Jerry] I think I'm the luckiest boy
in New York this morning.

34
00:01:46,356 --> 00:01:49,067
[Bridget] All right. We're gonna
light this town on fire. See you soon.

35
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:51,569
-[Jerry] I'll be there in a minute. Bye.
-All right. Bye.

36
00:01:51,653 --> 00:01:55,031
[Jerry] She really is a favorite,
favorite person of mine.

37
00:01:55,115 --> 00:01:57,492
And that's why I am Jerry Seinfeld,

38
00:01:57,575 --> 00:02:00,829
and this
is Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

39
00:02:02,455 --> 00:02:04,457
-Now, is Poppy coming with us?
-Yeah.

40
00:02:04,541 --> 00:02:07,127
-Where is she?
-Oh, she's in the bag. [laughs]

41
00:02:07,210 --> 00:02:09,587
-Oh, my God! Look at this!
-[man] Oh, nice.

42
00:02:09,671 --> 00:02:11,673
-This is show biz.
-Yeah, right?

43
00:02:12,215 --> 00:02:16,386
[Bridget] This is like the kind of car
I always dreamed of being in and having.

44
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:21,724
This show is about cars
that are fantastic to photograph

45
00:02:21,808 --> 00:02:23,476
and a bitch to drive!

46
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:24,686
[Bridget laughs]

47
00:02:24,769 --> 00:02:25,979
This car rides pretty nice.

48
00:02:26,062 --> 00:02:27,397
-It does.
-I'm kind of enjoying it.

49
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:28,648
Right? This is nice.

50
00:02:28,731 --> 00:02:31,651
Do you feel a lot pressure
to not... crash?

51
00:02:31,734 --> 00:02:34,028
I do feel pressure not to crash.

52
00:02:34,112 --> 00:02:36,781
But I love wheels.

53
00:02:36,865 --> 00:02:39,284
-I love wheels. I love that they turn.
-I love wheels, too.

54
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:41,369
-Yeah.
-And that you roll on them.

55
00:02:41,452 --> 00:02:45,123
I love skateboards, and bicycles,
and motorcycles.

56
00:02:45,206 --> 00:02:48,418
-I can't contain my enthusiasm.
-Good.

57
00:02:48,501 --> 00:02:49,961
I think when Larry David

58
00:02:50,545 --> 00:02:52,922
named his show Curb Your Enthusiasm...

59
00:02:53,006 --> 00:02:55,633
-[Bridget laughs]
-...I think he was talking about me.

60
00:02:55,717 --> 00:02:57,427
[Bridget laughs]

61
00:02:58,344 --> 00:03:00,638
I think that was a direct comment to me.

62
00:03:01,890 --> 00:03:04,642
My dad had a '63 Caddy  that he loved,

63
00:03:04,726 --> 00:03:05,894
-a Fleetwood.
-Uh-huh.

64
00:03:05,977 --> 00:03:07,687
His dream was always the Caddy.

65
00:03:07,770 --> 00:03:10,690
My mom was always like, "You're really
doing good if got a Cadillac."

66
00:03:10,773 --> 00:03:11,608
Yeah.

67
00:03:11,858 --> 00:03:13,568
But, um... we had a Chevette.

68
00:03:14,652 --> 00:03:16,154
There was one time that we drove

69
00:03:16,237 --> 00:03:19,157
all the way down to Florida
in a Chevette from Kansas.

70
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:21,409
My mom, my brother, and I,
'cause my great-aunt died.

71
00:03:21,701 --> 00:03:24,579
We go pick her up at the funeral home,

72
00:03:25,288 --> 00:03:28,041
and, um, they're like,
"Uh, just give us another 15 minutes.

73
00:03:28,124 --> 00:03:29,876
She isn't quite ready." [laughs]

74
00:03:30,335 --> 00:03:33,504
So, they hand us the tube,
and she was still hot to the touch.

75
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:37,467
It was so disgusting, Jerry. [laughs]

76
00:03:37,550 --> 00:03:39,219
That is a little real.

77
00:03:39,302 --> 00:03:41,304
But that's when you're glad
that you have a Chevette,

78
00:03:41,387 --> 00:03:43,056
'cause you can put her in the hatchback.

79
00:03:43,598 --> 00:03:45,475
And she rolled around, she cooled off.

80
00:03:47,185 --> 00:03:49,312
I'm not used to people
being so nice to me.

81
00:03:49,395 --> 00:03:52,273
Everyone's nice to you.
I've never seen anyone not...

82
00:03:52,982 --> 00:03:55,568
adore every second 
when they get with you.

83
00:03:55,652 --> 00:03:57,487
-People are very nice.
-You're a get.

84
00:03:57,570 --> 00:03:59,030
You're a get now.

85
00:03:59,113 --> 00:04:00,740
-I'm a get now.
-Yeah.

86
00:04:00,823 --> 00:04:03,493
Well, that's part of your charm,
is you kind of play it off.

87
00:04:03,576 --> 00:04:05,495
You know, that, "Oh, I'm just Bridget

88
00:04:05,578 --> 00:04:07,497
-from Kansas."
-[laughs]

89
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:09,415
-So, I had to use this show.
-That's funny.

90
00:04:09,499 --> 00:04:11,584
-I used my show to get you.
-[laughs]

91
00:04:12,543 --> 00:04:15,255
I remember one time you came to see
this pilot I did,

92
00:04:15,338 --> 00:04:16,965
and then you texted me the next day.

93
00:04:17,048 --> 00:04:19,425
-Yeah.
-And I just stared at my phone for, like,

94
00:04:19,801 --> 00:04:20,635
20 minutes.

95
00:04:20,718 --> 00:04:22,804
And you're like, "Let's get lunch."
I'm like, "Lunch!

96
00:04:22,887 --> 00:04:23,972
[Jerry laughing]

97
00:04:24,055 --> 00:04:25,431
Where? Why?"

98
00:04:25,515 --> 00:04:26,933
[Jerry laughing]

99
00:04:27,976 --> 00:04:30,687
Did you have a lot of years of struggle
or were you just always like...

100
00:04:30,770 --> 00:04:32,522
-No.
-[laughs]

101
00:04:35,441 --> 00:04:39,070
Oh, I think my favorite thing about you
is how you just, "No."

102
00:04:39,153 --> 00:04:40,363
[laughs]

103
00:04:40,446 --> 00:04:42,323
-Well, let me be clear.
-Yeah.

104
00:04:42,407 --> 00:04:44,284
-When you're 22 years old...
-Yeah.

105
00:04:44,367 --> 00:04:47,954
...and you're making $65 a week,

106
00:04:48,037 --> 00:04:49,414
-you're good.
-Yeah.

107
00:04:49,497 --> 00:04:52,917
All you need is two pairs of jeans
and some Tide.

108
00:04:53,001 --> 00:04:54,085
[laughs]

109
00:04:55,003 --> 00:04:57,672
-[Bridget] I went to Arizona State.
-I've just read that this morning.

110
00:04:57,755 --> 00:05:00,049
-And they said you had a full scholarship.
-Yeah.

111
00:05:00,133 --> 00:05:01,301
I studied Opera.

112
00:05:01,384 --> 00:05:03,386
♪ I'd like to slap their faces! ♪

113
00:05:03,594 --> 00:05:06,014
♪ What do they think they are? ♪

114
00:05:06,306 --> 00:05:08,683
Do you... do you follow opera? [laughs]

115
00:05:08,766 --> 00:05:09,767
Not closely.

116
00:05:10,852 --> 00:05:12,478
[Bridget] What happens out here?

117
00:05:12,812 --> 00:05:13,646
Here?

118
00:05:13,730 --> 00:05:16,482
Yeah. Like, did you pick this
for a reason, this part of town?

119
00:05:16,566 --> 00:05:18,067
-Or this town?
-Well, it could be nice.

120
00:05:18,359 --> 00:05:19,694
[laughs]

121
00:05:20,153 --> 00:05:22,322
But you don't visit it on occasion?

122
00:05:23,072 --> 00:05:24,699
-Me?
-This Jersey town. Yeah.

123
00:05:25,283 --> 00:05:26,743
-Me come here?
-Yeah.

124
00:05:27,243 --> 00:05:28,077
No.

125
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:32,457
See, there's just a type of person
that can live here.

126
00:05:32,832 --> 00:05:34,083
-There is.
-You're not that type.

127
00:05:34,167 --> 00:05:35,835
-Here's my theory, okay?
-Okay.

128
00:05:35,918 --> 00:05:39,047
-We all need a certain tension level...
-Yeah.

129
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:42,342
...that is comfortable,
that matches what some kind of...

130
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,845
uh, harmonic vibration that you have.

131
00:05:45,928 --> 00:05:47,221
-Yeah.
-Right?

132
00:05:47,305 --> 00:05:48,639
-Yeah.
-So, that's where you live.

133
00:05:48,723 --> 00:05:50,308
-That's where you feel comfortable.
-Yes.

134
00:05:50,391 --> 00:05:54,187
I need that tuning fork
to be humming at this pitch.

135
00:05:54,270 --> 00:05:57,190
That's so true.
And if it goes, like, slightly

136
00:05:57,565 --> 00:06:00,568
left-of-center or right-of-center,
I really...

137
00:06:00,902 --> 00:06:02,862
-I lash out. [laughs]
-Yeah.

138
00:06:03,988 --> 00:06:06,324
Oh, look at that old car dealer.

139
00:06:06,657 --> 00:06:08,284
I like everything on that lot.

140
00:06:10,036 --> 00:06:11,329
Look, Tina Fey!

141
00:06:11,412 --> 00:06:12,413
This is on the side.

142
00:06:12,747 --> 00:06:14,749
Oh, it says "Tena Fly. Oh, Tenafly."

143
00:06:14,832 --> 00:06:16,084
[laughing]

144
00:06:16,709 --> 00:06:18,795
Your mom would think,
"That must be Tina Fey's house."

145
00:06:18,878 --> 00:06:19,879
[laughing] Yeah.

146
00:06:27,637 --> 00:06:28,513
Good girl.

147
00:06:30,264 --> 00:06:31,099
[Jerry sneezes]

148
00:06:31,724 --> 00:06:33,393
-Bless you.
-[Jerry sneezes]

149
00:06:34,394 --> 00:06:36,354
-[Jerry inhales, sneezes]
-Bless you.

150
00:06:38,856 --> 00:06:41,526
That is the most interesting sneeze
I've ever heard.

151
00:06:41,609 --> 00:06:42,652
It's tender and...

152
00:06:43,194 --> 00:06:46,197
and full of rage all at the same time.
[laughing]

153
00:06:47,323 --> 00:06:48,908
I always want people to have...

154
00:06:49,367 --> 00:06:51,119
-a real experience, and like...
-Yes.

155
00:06:51,577 --> 00:06:53,830
I like it when people walk out. 
You know, I don't love it,

156
00:06:53,913 --> 00:06:56,082
but I like that they're having,
like, a reaction

157
00:06:56,165 --> 00:06:58,334
and they just, like,
they got to get out of there.

158
00:07:00,336 --> 00:07:02,004
I lived in the same shitty apartment

159
00:07:02,088 --> 00:07:04,173
-for 18 years on the ground floor...
-Wow.

160
00:07:04,257 --> 00:07:06,551
...which, I think
that might be largely why

161
00:07:06,634 --> 00:07:08,636
-I was just depressed all the time.
-Yeah, right.

162
00:07:08,719 --> 00:07:12,140
I don't know, I think that that's what
makes doing shows so exciting.

163
00:07:12,223 --> 00:07:14,934
-You got a reason to live and be happy.
-Yes!

164
00:07:16,352 --> 00:07:18,688
I did have a really good time
with you one time,

165
00:07:18,771 --> 00:07:20,648
-and you had a couple glasses of wine.
 -Yeah!

166
00:07:20,731 --> 00:07:21,899
That was delightful.

167
00:07:22,483 --> 00:07:24,944
When you get Jerry Seinfeld
a couple glasses of wine in him,

168
00:07:25,027 --> 00:07:26,571
you're gonna have a real good time.

169
00:07:26,988 --> 00:07:28,281
[Jerry] Know what's really funny?

170
00:07:28,364 --> 00:07:31,409
Is to have a couple glasses of wine
and be around one of your kids.

171
00:07:31,492 --> 00:07:33,453
-[laughs]
-'Cause they kind of freak out.

172
00:07:33,536 --> 00:07:34,537
Do they?

173
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:37,457
"What's with Dad?" 
You know, it's really fun.

174
00:07:38,916 --> 00:07:40,793
[Bridget] I was watching those, um...

175
00:07:40,877 --> 00:07:43,129
Dean Martin Roasts.
I  thought of my dad as Dean Martin,

176
00:07:43,212 --> 00:07:45,298
-but he wasn't as suave, you know, but...
-Yeah.

177
00:07:45,381 --> 00:07:47,091
And, um... Oh, Foster Brooks.

178
00:07:47,175 --> 00:07:49,260
-I think we talked about Foster Brooks.
-Yeah, we did.

179
00:07:49,802 --> 00:07:52,472
I have never met Don Rickles.

180
00:07:52,555 --> 00:07:54,140
[laughter]

181
00:07:54,599 --> 00:07:57,226
However, Mr. Rickles, you and I do have...

182
00:07:58,352 --> 00:08:00,855
an indirect relationship.

183
00:08:00,938 --> 00:08:02,356
[laughter]

184
00:08:02,440 --> 00:08:04,567
You see, I'm fooling around
with your wife.

185
00:08:04,859 --> 00:08:05,985
[laughter]

186
00:08:09,030 --> 00:08:12,450
Now, you know what he used to do
before he was discovered by Dean Martin?

187
00:08:12,533 --> 00:08:14,869
-Was what?
-He used to just do corporate gigs,

188
00:08:15,369 --> 00:08:16,787
and they would introduce him

189
00:08:17,121 --> 00:08:20,124
as the CEO of the company
we're considering merging with.

190
00:08:20,208 --> 00:08:21,417
[laughs]

191
00:08:21,501 --> 00:08:22,752
[both laugh]

192
00:08:24,045 --> 00:08:25,838
Did your parents
used to have cocktail parties?

193
00:08:25,922 --> 00:08:28,466
-No...
-Mom and Dad had these cocktail parties.

194
00:08:28,883 --> 00:08:31,260
-Everybody-- Jews...
-Jews don't have cocktail parties.

195
00:08:31,344 --> 00:08:32,762
They order out Chinese.

196
00:08:34,055 --> 00:08:35,223
[Jerry] I love Chinese food.

197
00:08:35,306 --> 00:08:36,849
-I do, too.
-I just want to go to China

198
00:08:36,933 --> 00:08:39,769
and go, "What's a good Chinese--
Is there a good Chinese restaurant here?"

199
00:08:39,852 --> 00:08:42,939
"We don't have Chinese restaurants.
There's just restaurants."

200
00:08:43,022 --> 00:08:44,065
[Bridget laughs]

201
00:08:45,066 --> 00:08:46,859
[Bridget] Those windows
look like milk bottles.

202
00:08:46,943 --> 00:08:48,277
-Don't they?
-They do.

203
00:08:49,487 --> 00:08:52,073
I got to find more...
more hobbies, more interests.

204
00:08:52,156 --> 00:08:53,866
-Why?
-I don't know.

205
00:08:53,950 --> 00:08:56,536
You're assuming that the world
is interesting. It's not.

206
00:08:57,828 --> 00:09:00,164
[Jerry] So, who was
the first person you saw

207
00:09:00,790 --> 00:09:01,791
when you were a kid?

208
00:09:02,416 --> 00:09:05,628
John Belushi on Saturday Night Live
and Animal House.

209
00:09:05,711 --> 00:09:07,171
Like, I just felt, like,

210
00:09:07,588 --> 00:09:09,924
-so electrified by him.
-Yeah.

211
00:09:10,007 --> 00:09:13,177
Really, really sad,
like, when he died and...

212
00:09:13,261 --> 00:09:15,721
Sometimes it's sad,
and sometimes it's beautiful.

213
00:09:15,805 --> 00:09:17,265
-Yeah.
-They did...

214
00:09:17,723 --> 00:09:19,600
get it out there for us.

215
00:09:19,684 --> 00:09:22,186
-Yeah. Yeah.
-That crazy thing that they had.

216
00:09:22,895 --> 00:09:24,730
There was no other way for that to go.

217
00:09:24,814 --> 00:09:27,942
You know, I'm good friends with [bleep],
and that's his...

218
00:09:28,442 --> 00:09:30,611
-I don't like him.
-His friend. At all.

219
00:09:30,695 --> 00:09:32,280
-Oh, no. [laughs]
-At all.

220
00:09:32,697 --> 00:09:35,283
-In fact, I have a particular...
-[laughing]

221
00:09:35,366 --> 00:09:37,743
-...feeling about him.
-Oh, God.

222
00:09:37,827 --> 00:09:39,870
-I had kind of forgotten about him.
-Mm-hmm.

223
00:09:39,954 --> 00:09:42,915
And then there was a little article
about him in the paper.

224
00:09:43,374 --> 00:09:46,294
And even in that,
there was a veiled reference

225
00:09:46,377 --> 00:09:48,713
to his dislike of what I did.

226
00:09:49,755 --> 00:09:51,924
-Really?
-It didn't have my name, of course.

227
00:09:52,008 --> 00:09:53,801
He used to rail against me,

228
00:09:53,884 --> 00:09:56,012
'cause they weren't as wild and dangerous

229
00:09:56,095 --> 00:09:56,971
-as he was.
-Yeah.

230
00:09:57,054 --> 00:09:58,014
'Cause he sucked.

231
00:09:58,306 --> 00:09:59,307
[laughs]

232
00:09:59,932 --> 00:10:01,517
Okay? He wasn't funny.

233
00:10:01,601 --> 00:10:03,853
-Yeah.
-And that's why he didn't get anywhere.

234
00:10:03,936 --> 00:10:05,313
-Yeah.
-Period.

235
00:10:06,355 --> 00:10:09,025
-This feels-- I feel very tense right now.
-He sucked.

236
00:10:09,442 --> 00:10:11,193
-'Cause in comedy...
-Yeah.

237
00:10:11,277 --> 00:10:15,156
...nobody gives a [bleep]
if you're cool, if you're lame.

238
00:10:15,239 --> 00:10:17,033
If you're funny, you win.

239
00:10:17,116 --> 00:10:19,577
